<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="cellulose biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-1001" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: CELLULOSE &quot;Cellulose&quot;| is the most abundant organic polymer found in nature. It is produced by plants, some animals, algae, fungi, flagellates and bacteria such as |FRAME: TAX-28448|, |FRAME: TAX-358| and |FRAME: TAX-379| spp. |CITS: [2030672]|. Cellulose is an intrinsic structural component of plant primary and secondary cell walls, building a network with other carbohydrate polymers. Bacteria generally produce cellulose as an extracellular component for mechanical and chemical protection, but it can also be used to facilitate cell adhesion to host tissue |CITS: [7462151]|. Cellulose accounts for about 20% (by weight) of the plant primary cell walls, and about 50% of the secondary cell walls. The primary structure of cellulose is a linear array of D-glucose molecules joined by &amp;beta; (1,4) glycosyl linkage. The biosynthesis of cellulose is catalyzed by the cellulose synthase enzyme complex located in the plasma membrane. Polymer synthesis is coupled to membrane translocation through a channel formed by the cellulose synthase. The enzyme is visualized as rosettes that are composed of six subunits, with each rosette subunit in turn composed of 6 cellulose synthases. Each individual cellulose synthase catalyzes the formation of one glucan chain which then associates with neighbor chains of the same rosette to form cellulose microfibrils |CITS: [12514238]|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; It was hypothesized that cellulose synthesis requires a primer, similar to glycogen biosynthesis. Work in cotton has shown that |FRAME: CPD-11608| can serve as a primer. UDP-glucose can be used to form sterol cellodextrins from |FRAME: CPD-11608|, presumably catalyzed by a cellulose synthase |CITS: [11778054]|. Eukaryotic cellulose synthases function in macromolecular complexes containing several different enzyme isoforms. At least six cellulose synthase genes were shown to be required in cellulose synthesis in |FRAME: TAX-3701|. |FRAME: G-1681|, |FRAME: G-1682|, and |FRAME: G-1683| are involved in cellulose biosynthesis in the primary walls. |FRAME: G-1684|, |FRAME: G-1685|, and |FRAME: G-1686| are involved in cellulose synthesis in the secondary walls. Mutant and mutant complementation analysis indicate that these isoforms of cellulose synthase are not functionally redundant. The UDP-glucose is channeled in Arabidopsis from sucrose degradation. It has been shown in Arabidopsis that in addition to cellulose synthase |FRAME: MONOMER-2367| is also required in cellulose biosynthesis. It is possibly involved in trimming off defective glucans from the growing microfibrils, or cleaving off the sitosterol-&amp;beta;-glucose primer from the growing glucan chains |CITS: [11351091]|. Bacterial cellulose is implicated in the formation of biofilms, and its synthesis is stimulated by cyclic-di-GMP. Prokaryotic cellulose synthases associate with additional subunits to bridge the periplasm and the outer membrane. In bacteria, cellulose synthesis and translocation is catalyzed by the inner membrane-associated bacterial cellulose synthase BcsA and BcsB subunits. BcsA is the catalytically active subunit, and BcsB is a membrane-anchored subunit that is essential for catalysis.</Description>
